"""
Tutorial 3 — Design the continuous LQG controller.

`LQGAnalysis` opens the measurement and control points before it designs the controller.
No extra loop openings are needed here.

Choose each weight in the units of its signal:
- `q1_diag`: penalize output errors.
- `q2_diag`: penalize control effort.
- `r1_diag`: set the assumed input disturbance variances.
- `r2_diag`: set the assumed sensor noise variances. Larger values mean less trust.

Cost weights use `1 / deviation^2`. Noise variances use `standard deviation^2`.
Measured angles use radians and rates use rad/s. Surface commands use degrees.
Thrust uses newtons. The small position weights let the aircraft move forward.
Control weights discourage large commands but do not enforce actuator limits.

Show 30 seconds of the step response. An automatic duration can be very long
because the position modes are slow.
"""
analysis TutorialLQG
  extends DyadControlSystems.LQGAnalysis(measurement = ["yn", "ye", "yalt", "yphi", "ypitch", "ypsi", "yvt", "yalpha", "ybeta", "yP", "yQ", "yR"], controlled_output = ["yn", "ye", "yalt", "ypitch", "yvt", "yalpha", "ybeta", "yQ"], control_input = ["uT", "uEl", "uAil", "uRud", "uLef"], loop_openings = [], q1_diag = [1e-6, 1e-6, 4.0, 131.312, 1.0, 820.702, 820.702, 32.8281], q2_diag = [1.1111111e-7, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01], r1_diag = [40000.0, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25], r2_diag = [2500.0, 2500.0, 0.01, 1.2184697e-5, 1.2184697e-5, 1.2184697e-5, 0.0025, 3.0461742e-6, 3.0461742e-6, 7.6154355e-7, 7.6154355e-7, 7.6154355e-7], duration = 30.0)
  model = LQGDemo()
relations
end

"""
Tutorial 3b — Design the 100 Hz LQG controller for step 4.

Convert the plant to discrete time using a zero-order hold, then design the controller.
Use `ControllerTs` as the sample period. Keep the weights in sync with `TutorialLQG`.
"""
analysis TutorialDiscreteLQG
  extends DyadControlSystems.LQGAnalysis(measurement = ["yn", "ye", "yalt", "yphi", "ypitch", "ypsi", "yvt", "yalpha", "ybeta", "yP", "yQ", "yR"], controlled_output = ["yn", "ye", "yalt", "ypitch", "yvt", "yalpha", "ybeta", "yQ"], control_input = ["uT", "uEl", "uAil", "uRud", "uLef"], loop_openings = [], q1_diag = [1e-6, 1e-6, 4.0, 131.312, 1.0, 820.702, 820.702, 32.8281], q2_diag = [1.1111111e-7, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01], r1_diag = [40000.0, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25], r2_diag = [2500.0, 2500.0, 0.01, 1.2184697e-5, 1.2184697e-5, 1.2184697e-5, 0.0025, 3.0461742e-6, 3.0461742e-6, 7.6154355e-7, 7.6154355e-7, 7.6154355e-7], duration = 30.0, disc = "zoh", Ts = ControllerTs)
  model = LQGDemo()
relations
end

"""
Tutorial 3c — save the continuous LQG controller.

Runs `TutorialLQG` and writes its controller to `assets/controller.toml`, which `LQGDemo`
and `ContinuousClosedLoopDemo` (step 5) apply. Run it after changing the design.
"""
analysis TutorialLQGExport
  extends F16ModelWorkshop.ControllerExportAnalysis(design = "TutorialLQG", file = "assets/controller.toml", add_trim = false)
  model = LQGDemo()
end

"""
Tutorial 3d — save the 100 Hz LQG controller for steps 4–6.

Runs `TutorialDiscreteLQG` and writes its controller, with the trim controls as its
output offset `y0`, to `assets/discrete_controller.toml`. Run it after changing the design.
"""
analysis TutorialDiscreteLQGExport
  extends F16ModelWorkshop.ControllerExportAnalysis(design = "TutorialDiscreteLQG", file = "assets/discrete_controller.toml", add_trim = true)
  model = LQGDemo()
end
